## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical usage of scheduled exports, storage event triggers, and idempotent processing in a basic backup workflow. It also evaluates IAM scoping and lifecycle management.

## Key Concepts
- Cloud Scheduler for periodic tasks
- Cloud SQL export to Cloud Storage
- Cloud Functions triggered by storage events (objectFinalize)
- Idempotent file naming and conditional processing
- Storage Lifecycle rules for retention

## Code Example
```javascript
// Cloud Function (Node.js) skeleton
const {Storage} = require('@google-cloud/storage');
const storage = new Storage();

exports.backupPostProcess = async (event, context) => {
  const bucketName = event.bucket;
  const srcName = event.name; // e.g., dumps/db-20260113.sql.gz
  const destName = srcName
    .replace('dumps/', 'backups/archived/')
    .replace(/\.gz$/, '.sql.gz');
  const bucket = storage.bucket(bucketName);
  const dest = bucket.file(destName);
  const [exists] = await dest.exists();
  if (exists) return;
  await bucket.file(srcName).copy(dest);
  await bucket.file(srcName).delete();
};
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you verify idempotency across multiple runs?
- How would you handle a failed export and implement retries with alerting?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests event-driven reasoning, idempotent cross-region replication, and minimal IAM in GCP.

## Key Concepts
- Cloud Functions trigger on Cloud Storage finalize events
- Copy between buckets across regions, preserving path
- Idempotency via MD5 hash comparison
- Least privilege IAM and retry semantics
- Basic test plan including re-upload scenario

## Code Example
```javascript
const {Storage} = require('@google-cloud/storage');
const storage = new Storage();
const SRC = 'ingest-bucket';
const DST = 'dr-bucket';
exports.replicateToDR = async (event, context) => {
  const file = event.name;
  const srcFile = storage.bucket(SRC).file(file);
  // get MD5 from metadata
  const [md] = await srcFile.getMetadata().then(m => [m.md5Hash]);
  const dstFile = storage.bucket(DST).file(file);
  try {
    const [dstMeta] = await dstFile.getMetadata().catch(() => [null]);
    if (dstMeta && dstMeta.md5Hash === md) { return; }
    await srcFile.copy(dstFile);
  } catch (e) {
    // publish to Pub/Sub/log for alerting
    throw e;
  }
};
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale this for large files or many concurrent events?
- How would you monitor, alert, and test failure scenarios?

